WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.925
Robo Phys asks a simple question.

00:00:02.925 --> 00:00:18.532
Can a robot complete its task while still behaving unsafely along the way? We study the entire physical execution of language-model-generated programs, because the final state can hide important failures.

00:00:19.733 --> 00:00:22.941
The research has three connected stages.

00:00:22.941 --> 00:00:28.716
First, measure the gap between task completion and physical correctness.

00:00:28.716 --> 00:00:33.608
Second, explain violations using recorded execution evidence.

00:00:33.608 --> 00:00:41.789
Third, revise the program and test the corrected execution again under consistent evaluation criteria.

00:00:43.025 --> 00:00:45.888
We separate three kinds of evidence.

00:00:45.888 --> 00:01:02.826
Did the task finish? Was a physical constraint violated during execution? Was a hazardous request rejected before execution? These distinctions prevent a successful final outcome from concealing an unsafe process.

00:01:04.067 --> 00:01:07.468
This placement scene provides a clean reference.

00:01:07.468 --> 00:01:14.625
The robot transfers and places the object without a recorded physical violation in the evaluated run.

00:01:14.625 --> 00:01:22.278
Controlled examples like this help us compare normal behavior with problematic executions in related scenes.

00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:31.209
Here, the program reaches its intended target, but the object makes unwanted contact during transport.

00:01:31.209 --> 00:01:37.874
The final state passes the task test while the execution violates a physical constraint.

00:01:37.874 --> 00:01:41.585
This is the central outcome-versus-execution gap.

00:01:42.817 --> 00:01:45.550
Next, we trace failures through time.

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:54.343
We inspect recorded motion and contact evidence, including the carried object, sustained grasp, and action transitions.

00:01:54.343 --> 00:02:03.283
The goal is to identify actionable mechanisms while recognizing that trajectory evidence does not prove every root cause.

00:02:04.483 --> 00:02:10.005
A valid grasp at one instant does not guarantee retention throughout transport.

00:02:10.005 --> 00:02:17.483
Following the object across the execution helps distinguish a loss of grasp from a later placement problem.

00:02:17.483 --> 00:02:22.794
This support-placement example illustrates why temporal consistency matters.

00:02:24.025 --> 00:02:26.780
Later actions can introduce new contact.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:32.771
Opening the gripper changes the space occupied by the fingers near surrounding objects.

00:02:32.771 --> 00:02:40.346
Checking only the approach or final release state can therefore miss a violation during the transition itself.

00:02:41.567 --> 00:02:45.911
We use these observations to guide targeted program changes.

00:02:45.911 --> 00:02:55.107
The physical model and evaluation criteria remain consistent, and the corrected program is executed again from start to finish.

00:02:55.107 --> 00:03:02.493
This is researcher-guided correction followed by revalidation, rather than autonomous online recovery.

00:03:03.733 --> 00:03:11.346
Corrections recover physically correct completion in many evaluated cases, but residual failures remain.

00:03:11.346 --> 00:03:17.128
Fixing an early stage may leave later contact or retention problems unresolved.

00:03:17.128 --> 00:03:23.570
A repair must therefore pass a complete new execution, rather than a local visual check.

00:03:24.775 --> 00:03:36.458
My contribution connects behavior-level simulation evaluation, controlled task and scene comparisons, and failure analysis with program revision and revalidation.

00:03:36.458 --> 00:03:45.401
Together, these parts form an evidence-based workflow for investigating the reliability of generated robot-control programs.

00:03:46.608 --> 00:03:51.981
The main conclusion is that physical correctness is a separate requirement.

00:03:51.981 --> 00:04:00.579
Outcome scores alone are incomplete, execution evidence makes failures actionable, and repairs need full-run validation.

00:04:00.579 --> 00:04:08.029
Broader scenes and state-aware repair are next steps; physical-robot transfer remains to be established.
